	// Oxlint was designed to be a linter that is
	// - zero-config out of the box; even Node.js is not a requirement.
	// - only checks for correctness errors (code that is outright wrong or useless) - definition from Rust Clippy
	//
	// Reference: https://github.com/oxc-project/oxc/issues/481
	//
	// Oxlint defaults to identifying erroneous, redundant, or confusing code — prioritizing correctness over unnecessary
	// nitpicking rules (categorized as perf, suspicious, pedantic, or style), which are disabled by default.
	//
	// Setting up new JavaScript / TypeScript codebases is becoming increasingly complex. There's a high likelihood of
	// encountering compatibility issues among your tools, potentially resulting in hours of wasted time.
	//
	// Reference: https://oxc.rs/blog/2023-12-12-announcing-oxlint.html
	//
	// While no setup or configuration is needed, Oxlint is configurable via an .oxlintrc.json file, which is useful for larger
	// projects or projects that require more customization. This configuration format is based on ESLint v8’s flat config,
	// making migration easy and familiar.
	//
	// For projects already using ESLint, oxlint-migrate can be used to migrate an existing ESLint flat-config file to Oxlint.
	// Additionally, eslint-plugin-oxlint can disable overlapping ESLint rules while both linters are used together. It is
	// recommended to run oxlint && eslint to benefit from Oxlint's faster feedback cycle.
	//
	// Reference: https://oxc.rs/blog/2025-06-10-oxlint-stable.html

	"$schema": "./node_modules/oxlint/configuration_schema.json",

	// Reference: https://oxc.rs/docs/guide/usage/linter/plugins.html#supported-plugins
	"plugins": [
		// Default plugins
		"eslint",
		"typescript",
		"oxc",
		"unicorn",

		// Project-specific plugins
		"import"
	],

